  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather prevails for the rest of this week\nwith moderating temperatures. Patchy frost may occur in the \nGreenbrier River Valley and at Burkes Garden during Tuesday and \nWednesday.\n\nHigh pressure should build across the Appalachian Mountains\nlater this evening into tonight. Cloud cover will gradually \ndissipate as a northwest breeze gusting up to 20 mph advects \ndrier air from Canada. With winds becoming lighter overnight \namidst clear skies and falling dewpoints, temperatures may fall \ninto the mid to upper 30s to promote patchy frost in the \nGreenbrier River Valley and at Burkes Garden. Elsewhere, \ntemperatures should reach the 40s. Patchy river valley fog could\ndevelop early Tuesday morning west of the Blue Ridge, while a \nthin layer of moisture gets trapped under the subsidence \ninversion along and south of Route 460 that could produce \nscattered low clouds.\n\nAfter a chilly Tuesday morning, temperatures should moderate as\nthe center of high pressure reaches the East Coast. The flow \nwill turn towards the southwest during Wednesday and Thursday to\nboost temperatures into the 70s and 80s. A frontal passage to \nthe north late Thursday could bring slightly cooler air by \nFriday, but another area of high pressure should keep conditions\ndry through Saturday. The lack of rainfall this week will only \nworsen the ongoing drought across the Piedmont. Although \nconfidence remains low at this time due to significant model \nspread for Sunday and beyond, a tropical disturbance in the Gulf\nmight provide the next chance of rain.",
